1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,460
A desk breaking in Control

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:06,140
combines large planks, small fragments and dust.

3
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:07,380
To create this response,

4
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:10,460
calculating only the moment of impact is not enough.

5
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:11,740
Materials and fracture locations,

6
00:00:11,740 --> 00:00:14,360
along with their connections, need preparation.

7
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:16,340
The developers considered layers from whole objects

8
00:00:16,340 --> 00:00:19,460
to fragments and smaller particles.

9
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:20,980
Large pieces change the shape,

10
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:23,800
while small fragments and dust fill out the impact.

11
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:26,880
Even the smallest dust need not be treated

12
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:28,960
as an equally expensive simulated body.

13
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:32,660
Material data and rules reduced repetitive work.

14
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:35,880
Wood and glass should not fracture identically.

15
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:38,040
Define material-appropriate fracture methods,

16
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:41,560
then build a hierarchy of large and small fragments.

17
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:44,380
Pieces would scatter immediately if they were only separated.

18
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:47,840
Prepare connections between pieces and their strength.

19
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:51,040
Send the prepared pieces and physics data to the engine.

20
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:53,880
Then control what breaks away and what remains attached.

21
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:57,260
An impact breaks connections and physical pieces move.

22
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:01,180
Collisions and breakage also generate chips and dust.

23
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:03,540
Trigger effects appropriate to the material.

24
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:07,080
Large pieces and small effects communicate the impact together.

25
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:10,320
Marks left on surfaces also enrich the destruction.

26
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:11,700
But a mark drawn on a surface

27
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:14,220
is different from an actual geometric hole.

28
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:17,380
Control’s destruction links prepared fragments, bonds,

29
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:20,600
runtime physics and smaller visual traces.
